# EINORT-Commerce — test environment template.
#
# Copy to `.env.test` (gitignored). Loaded via `dotenv -e .env.test` by the
# `test:full` script; the Vitest `isolation` project refuses to start without it.

# --- Isolation-test database -------------------------------------------------
# A DEDICATED Neon branch, named `einort-test`, separate from the branch behind
# DATABASE_URL. `tests/setup/global-setup.ts` runs `prisma migrate deploy`
# against this URL and (from plan 01-04) truncates and reseeds it between runs —
# pointing it at a branch you care about will destroy that data.
#
# Create with: Neon console -> Branches -> New branch -> name it `einort-test`.
#
# Docker is not available on this machine, so Testcontainers is not an option;
# a persistent Neon branch is the sanctioned approach (01-RESEARCH.md
# "Environment Availability").
#
# Use the POOLED string for tests. Migrations inside global-setup run against
# this same URL.
#
# NOTE ON WHAT ACTUALLY IDENTIFIES THE BRANCH: a Neon connection string does
# NOT contain the branch name, and every branch of a project serves the same
# database name (`neondb`). Branches are distinguished only by their ENDPOINT ID
# — the `ep-<words>-<hash>` label. The `/einort-test` path below is illustrative
# only; the real branch URL ends in `/neondb` like the development one.
#
# That is why the destructive-seed guard in `tests/setup/seed-two-tenants.ts`
# allowlists endpoint IDs rather than grepping the URL for "einort-test": a
# substring guard would reject the real test branch every time.
TEST_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@ep-xxxx-pooler.REGION.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require"

# Comma-separated override for that endpoint allowlist. Only needed if the test
# branch is recreated (which changes its endpoint ID) and you would rather not
# edit DEFAULT_TEST_ENDPOINTS in tests/setup/seed-two-tenants.ts.
# TEST_DATABASE_ENDPOINTS="ep-xxxx"

# --- Optional overrides ------------------------------------------------------
# The isolation suite exercises the data layer, not the HTTP surface, so the
# app-level variables below are only needed if a test imports `@/env`.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ROOT_DOMAIN="localhost:3000"
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET="0000000000000000000000000000000000000000000"
# BETTER_AUTH_URL="http://localhost:3000"
